71
00:20:46,324 --> 00:20:50,503
So, you may be wondering what is this course about?

72
00:20:52,724 --> 00:20:57,804
This is a course that we will answer some questions.

73
00:20:59,324 --> 00:21:07,384
These questions, for example, what are the mathematical properties of computer hardware and software?

74
00:21:11,384 --> 00:21:13,304
What is computation?

75
00:21:14,291 --> 00:21:16,231
What is an algorithm?

76
00:21:18,011 --> 00:21:26,172
You may already come across to different algorithms in other courses, in other lectures,

77
00:21:26,791 --> 00:21:35,031
but probably that they gave you some informal definitions of algorithms.

78
00:21:35,031 --> 00:21:39,031
In this course, we will define...

79
00:21:39,347 --> 00:21:42,467
Algorithm, formally, mathematically.

80
00:21:44,667 --> 00:21:50,887
And also we will be interested in the limitations of computers.

81
00:21:52,067 --> 00:21:57,047
Can computers compute everything or not?

82
00:21:57,827 --> 00:22:02,087
And surprisingly, maybe the answer is no.

83
00:22:02,612 --> 00:22:07,932
Computers cannot compute every problem out there.

84
00:22:08,512 --> 00:22:10,472
And this is a theoretical result.

85
00:22:12,812 --> 00:22:26,771
So basically, we will be interested in developing formal mathematical models of computation.

86
00:22:27,412 --> 00:22:39,472
A part of the course is about complexity theory.

87
00:22:41,211 --> 00:22:52,172
And here the question is, what makes some problems computationally hard and some others easy?

88
00:22:52,819 --> 00:23:00,920
And again, we will define easy and hard mathematically.

89
00:23:02,539 --> 00:23:08,360
And as you maybe already know, some of the problems are easy.

90
00:23:08,360 --> 00:23:13,220
And easy problems can be efficiently solved.

91
00:23:13,779 --> 00:23:16,400
We can solve them fast.

92
00:23:17,460 --> 00:23:33,779
When the number of input is high, even if the number of the inputs are high, we can still solve these questions in reasonable time, very quickly.

93
00:23:35,220 --> 00:23:42,420
And these are, for example, sorting some numbers. This is an easy.

94
00:23:44,084 --> 00:23:45,684
Question or problem.

95
00:23:46,804 --> 00:23:50,224
Searching for a name in a list.

96
00:23:51,983 --> 00:24:02,384
Or computing the fastest drive from, for example, Esenler to Beşiktaş in İstanbul.

97
00:24:02,384 --> 00:24:06,184
So these are actually easy problems.

98
00:24:07,955 --> 00:24:23,555
When you have 1 million numbers, it's solvable. If you have 1 billion numbers, it's still solvable in a fast way, in a reasonable time.

99
00:24:25,475 --> 00:24:34,555
On the other hand, some of the problems are called hard. And these hard problems...

100
00:24:35,028 --> 00:24:43,148
When the input is large, we cannot solve them in reasonable time.

101
00:24:44,288 --> 00:24:50,188
So, for example, finding the timetable for all courses.

102
00:24:51,188 --> 00:25:00,748
For a single university, number of courses would be thousand or something, and today's computers are very fast.

103
00:25:01,523 --> 00:25:06,543
So, in a reasonable time, this can be solved.

104
00:25:06,663 --> 00:25:18,103
But let's say instead of thousand, let's say consider scheduling all the courses in the world, let's say.

105
00:25:18,864 --> 00:25:20,983
There should be millions of courses.

106
00:25:20,983 --> 00:25:25,563
And when the input increases like that,

107
00:25:25,903 --> 00:25:34,124
These hard questions become very, they take very long to solve.

108
00:25:34,423 --> 00:25:36,864
They cannot be solved in reasonable time.

109
00:25:39,684 --> 00:25:51,423
Another example for such questions for problems, for such problems is factoring a 300 integer into its prime factors.

110
00:25:52,019 --> 00:25:55,940
Or computing the layout for chips in VLSI.

111
00:25:57,719 --> 00:26:12,119
So, in this part of the course, in the complexity theory, the question is, which problems are difficult or hard?

112
00:26:13,579 --> 00:26:19,420
And some problems are hard, some problems are really hard.

113
00:26:20,819 --> 00:26:24,839
So, how can we define those?

114
00:26:25,519 --> 00:26:39,019
And that's important because if you know that a problem is hard, very hard to solve, theoretically, you can prepare for that.

115
00:26:39,119 --> 00:26:46,259
You will know that if the input is large, it will take a lot of time to solve.

116
00:26:47,028 --> 00:26:58,347
This thing, so maybe you go another way, maybe put the problem in another way, solve in another way.

117
00:26:59,288 --> 00:27:03,568
So understanding the problems is very important.

118
00:27:08,660 --> 00:27:14,980
Another part of the course is about compatibility theory.

119
00:27:15,860 --> 00:27:34,019
So, in this part, we will learn formal definitions of what is a computer, what is an algorithm,

120
00:27:34,804 --> 00:27:36,204
What is a computation?

121
00:27:41,644 --> 00:27:47,084
And also, some problems are unsolvable.

122
00:27:49,304 --> 00:27:56,124
So, we said that some problems are very hard.

123
00:27:58,224 --> 00:28:01,584
They are very hard, but they can somehow be

124
00:28:02,195 --> 00:28:09,755
Solt, but some problems will be unsolvable. Theoretically, we cannot solve them.

125
00:28:11,015 --> 00:28:20,815
And so, again, understanding whether a problem is solvable or not is very important.

126
00:28:20,815 --> 00:28:26,975
If the problem is not solvable with computers,

127
00:28:29,971 --> 00:28:49,071
Obviously, you will not try to write a program about it. You will not try to solve it, right? You will not spend time for it. Or you will try to approach the actual problem you have in a different way.

128
00:28:56,016 --> 00:28:59,955
Another part of the course is about automata theory.

129
00:28:59,955 --> 00:29:07,455
And actually, this will be in the beginning parts of the course.

130
00:29:07,455 --> 00:29:21,155
And this will give us about, we will talk about different computation models here.

131
00:29:24,564 --> 00:29:30,284
And these computation models will be very simple at first.

132
00:29:31,244 --> 00:29:35,724
Their capabilities will be low.

133
00:29:36,684 --> 00:29:45,424
Later, for example, first finite automata, these, their capabilities are low.

134
00:29:45,424 --> 00:29:48,604
Then we will learn context-free grammars.

135
00:29:49,324 --> 00:29:51,324
They will be a little more capable.

136
00:29:52,500 --> 00:29:55,819
And then we will see Turing machines.

137
00:29:56,419 --> 00:30:03,980
And Turing machines are theoretical models of real computers.

138
00:30:03,980 --> 00:30:08,720
So a Turing machine can do anything a real computer can do.

139
00:30:10,639 --> 00:30:19,079
So we will learn these computation models.

140
00:30:20,788 --> 00:30:30,128
And how can, so when you have a problem, which of these models can solve it?

141
00:30:32,368 --> 00:30:35,807
That's an important distinction, classification.

142
00:30:37,108 --> 00:30:45,348
And if you have a problem that can be solved by a finite automata, let's say you are building a machine.

143
00:30:46,867 --> 00:30:55,188
Let's say you are building a fridge or washing machine and you have a problem.

144
00:30:55,807 --> 00:31:08,728
And if you have a problem that can be solved by a finite automata, you can put a much cheaper intelligence in that machine.

145
00:31:09,523 --> 00:31:14,803
Because the requirement can be sold by a finite automata.

146
00:31:15,603 --> 00:31:24,443
But if your requirement is high, if your problem can be only sold by a Turing machine,

147
00:31:24,923 --> 00:31:35,443
that means you actually need something like a real computer, like a cell phone or like a...

148
00:31:36,019 --> 00:31:41,980
You need to put in a real chip or something.

149
00:31:42,139 --> 00:32:03,240
So if you understand the class of your problem, whether it can be solved by a finite automata or whether you can solve by a context-free grammar or by a Turing machine, you can use that information to create

150
00:32:03,700 --> 00:32:05,280
Cheap machines.

151
00:32:08,915 --> 00:32:10,875
among other users, obviously.

152
