160
00:33:29,971 --> 00:33:42,011
Mathematics is based on set theory, at least some of them, and this course is based on set theory too. Set theory is very important.

161
00:33:46,011 --> 00:33:50,432
Probably you know these things, but I will review them anyway.

162
00:33:52,596 --> 00:33:56,375
A set is a collection of well-defined objects.

163
00:33:57,635 --> 00:34:01,976
And some examples of sets are natural numbers.

164
00:34:03,276 --> 00:34:06,336
We show natural numbers with n.

165
00:34:07,135 --> 00:34:11,296
They start with 1 and go to infinity.

166
00:34:12,628 --> 00:34:25,748
In some books, or in some writers, mathematics writers, mathematicians, start natural numbers from zero.

167
00:34:27,028 --> 00:34:32,948
But in this course, zero will not be a natural number.

168
00:34:33,948 --> 00:34:35,948
Zero is not...

169
00:34:36,532 --> 00:34:42,832
It will be an unnatural number. Natural numbers will start from one in this course.

170
00:34:44,092 --> 00:34:56,252
And then another well-known set is integers. We will show that with Z, capital Z.

171
00:34:56,932 --> 00:35:03,332
And it starts from minus infinity and goes to...

172
00:35:04,563 --> 00:35:06,143
Positive infinity.

173
00:35:08,443 --> 00:35:15,443
Another set that is well known is set of rational numbers.

174
00:35:16,443 --> 00:35:20,983
We will show rational numbers with capital Q.

175
00:35:21,983 --> 00:35:30,403
Capital numbers are such numbers that we can represent it.

176
00:35:31,315 --> 00:35:35,416
as division of two integers.

177
00:35:37,015 --> 00:35:45,996
So, this set notation, you know this, but I will review it.

178
00:35:45,996 --> 00:35:47,916
How do we read this?

179
00:35:48,795 --> 00:35:57,275
Q is a set such that, so these two, this colon can be read as such that.

180
00:35:58,483 --> 00:36:04,203
Its representation is M divided by N.

181
00:36:05,983 --> 00:36:10,863
And M is in the set of integers.

182
00:36:11,863 --> 00:36:17,243
And so this comma can be read as N.

183
00:36:18,163 --> 00:36:21,363
So M is in the set of integers.

184
00:36:21,363 --> 00:36:22,163
And.

185
00:36:22,804 --> 00:36:26,204
N is in the set of integers.

186
00:36:27,903 --> 00:36:32,463
And N is not equal to zero.

187
00:36:33,463 --> 00:36:42,123
So if we can represent any number this way, we call it a rational number.

188
00:36:43,635 --> 00:36:53,296
So, for example, 3 is a rational number because 3 can be represented as 3 over 1, right?

189
00:36:54,376 --> 00:37:01,036
So, something like 2 over 3 is again a rational number.

190
00:37:03,315 --> 00:37:05,935
What are irrational?

191
00:37:06,435 --> 00:37:09,175
So, irrational numbers.

192
00:37:10,516 --> 00:37:21,916
The simplest way to define these is the set of numbers that are not rational, right?

193
00:37:21,916 --> 00:37:26,376
We can define them as not rational numbers.

194
00:37:27,235 --> 00:37:29,996
So pi, for example,

195
00:37:32,467 --> 00:37:54,207
The pi that we, or P, sorry, it's not pi, right? P. Somehow, from Python or something, P. So P is, P is an irrational number because we cannot represent P as division of two integers.

196
00:37:58,387 --> 00:38:01,007
Those are irrational numbers.

197
00:38:03,447 --> 00:38:10,807
Another well-known set is set of real numbers.

198
00:38:12,667 --> 00:38:17,307
There is a, it pronounced as pi.

199
00:38:17,307 --> 00:38:19,307
So pi was right.

200
00:38:23,155 --> 00:38:24,395
Yeah, okay.

201
00:38:25,175 --> 00:38:27,315
So, pi.

202
00:38:27,596 --> 00:38:29,715
So, let's continue to say pi.

203
00:38:29,876 --> 00:38:32,255
Thank you, [öğrenci], [öğrenci], [öğrenci].

204
00:38:34,515 --> 00:38:45,435
So, real numbers, the set of real numbers is denoted by R.

205
00:38:45,435 --> 00:38:49,996
And the definition of this,

206
00:38:50,867 --> 00:38:57,967
I don't remember whether we will give the definition of real numbers later in this course.

207
00:39:00,207 --> 00:39:01,287
Uncountable.

208
00:39:02,107 --> 00:39:04,627
This is an uncountable set.

209
00:39:05,987 --> 00:39:11,887
And it includes rational and irrational numbers.

210
00:39:17,876 --> 00:39:25,476
Something like pi. For example, pi, it should be a real number.

211
00:39:32,307 --> 00:39:44,788
Now, when we are talking about sets, we can talk about subsets of sets.

212
00:39:47,528 --> 00:39:59,788
For example, here, natural numbers n is a subset of integers because every element of...

213
00:40:00,019 --> 00:40:04,739
N is also an element of integers.

